        "original_id": "1cf7cf16-9f00-525d-b02d-70a97cac2909",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Eric Maskin, Leonid Hurwicz, Roger Myerson, Abraham Flexner, Louis Bamberger, Caroline Bamberger Fuld, Peter Sarnak, Charles Loewner, Mikhail Gromov, Michael Freedman, Mikhail Katz, Larry Guth\n- Organizations: Harvard University, Institute for Advanced Study, School of Mathematics\n- Places: Princeton, New Jersey, United States\n- Concepts: mechanism design theory, systolic geometry, systolic invariants\n\nFacts:\n- Eric Stark Maskin was born on December 12, 1950.\n- The Institute for Advanced Study was founded in 1930 by Abraham Flexner together with Louis Bamberger and Caroline Bamberger Fuld.\n- The Institute for Advanced Study is an independent postdoctoral research center for theoretical research and intellectual inquiry in Princeton, New Jersey, United States.\n- Eric Maskin is an American economist and a 2007 Nobel laureate recognized with Leonid Hurwicz and Roger Myerson for laying the foundations of mechanism design theory.\n- Eric Maskin is the Adams University Professor at Harvard University.\n- Peter Sarnak is on the permanent faculty of the School of Mathematics at the Institute for Advanced Study.\n- Systolic geometry is the study of systolic invariants of manifolds and polyhedra.\n- Systolic geometry was initially conceived by Charles Loewner and developed by Mikhail Gromov, Michael Freedman, Peter Sarnak, Mikhail Katz, Larry Guth, and others in arithmetical, ergodic, and topological forms.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
